38:- [Design Documents](#design-documents)
82:`HarnessOptions.telemetry` accepts a host-owned `TelemetryContext` from `@earendil-works/pi-telemetry`, with optional `propagation` and `metrics` capabilities; omitted, it is a no-op. `DURABLE_TELEMETRY_SCHEMA`, exported from the package root, documents its typed spans, events, and metrics. Attributes contain IDs and control metadata, never messages, arguments, or results; IDs point to the transcript.
95:- **Conversation**: a transcript. `root()` creates the root conversation on first use; you can create more and fork them. A `Conversation` handle holds no state; compare handles by `id`.
97:- **Commit**: an atomic write. `conversation.commit((tx) => ...)` can append entries, edit documents, and create tasks together; either all of it is stored or none of it.
98:- **Document**: typed JSON state stored next to the transcript and changed in commits. Built-in ones hold each conversation's agent choices (`pi.agent`), provider-facing session identity (`pi.provider`), running generation and tools (`pi.live`), queued submissions (`pi.inbox`), and spend (`pi.usage`).
127:Work interrupted by a crash or close stays pending. `resume()` starts the task scheduler; submitting or waiting starts it too. A process that only shows a session another one drives opens it with `readOnly: true`: open recovers nothing, and every write, `resume()`, and every call that would run tasks rejects with `ReadOnlySession`. That option covers the Harness, not the storage open before it: open JSONL storage with `openNodeJsonlStorage(directory, context, { readOnly: true })` too, since a writable JSONL open repairs files the writer may be appending to (see [Storage](#storage)). SQLite storage opens as usual: SQLite's file locks keep it safe beside the writer. Each conversation has its own persisted UUIDv7 in `pi.provider`, forwarded to pi-ai as `sessionId` for provider prompt-cache and session affinity. It survives reopen, retries, reset, compaction, and model changes; a child or fork receives a fresh identity. A legacy conversation receives and persists one before its first generation or compaction request.
330:- Nested results are not kept: they live in the caller's task documents (`NestedCallDoc`, one per nested call, keyed by its key, holding `{ taskId, result? }`) only until the caller settles. The nested call's task record keeps only a small receipt. A stored result is exactly what `executeTool()` returns, without the model's `output`, so an image or a `bash` tail is stored once.
366:The extension also adds a `codemode` prompt section listing the tools scripts can call, as TypeScript declarations within a token budget; scripts find the others with `searchTools()`, `describeTool()`, and `describeNamespace()`. Group tools by name prefix with `namespaces: [{ name, description, prefix }]`, for example an MCP server's `mcp__github__`. The result reads like the coding agent's: a header, the script's output, and on failure the calls already made; text past `max_output_tokens` is cut, with the full text in a temp file. Scripts also get `models.*`: the model catalog, `models.classify()`, and `models.generateImages()`, which run as nested calls of internal tools (`models: false` turns them off). On Cloudflare, pass a `RemoteCodemodeSandbox` instead. Closing the sandbox while a script runs (for example when shutting down) is not an outcome: the script resumes with the next process's sandbox. A failed nested call rejects in the script with its error text, unless the tool declares a schema and returned its own value with the error: `tools.bash` resolves with `{ output, exitCode, ... }` also for a nonzero exit, so scripts check `exitCode`. `store(key, value)` and `load(key)` keep JSON values per conversation; a fork sees them as they were at its entry.
372:The system prompt is built from the selected extensions' sections, rendered in order before each request. A section sees the resolved agent, the environment built for the request, and committed documents:
382:Each conversation stores what it runs with in its `pi.agent` document. `configure()` changes it in one commit; unset fields follow the host:
402:Extensions and tools are passed as objects and stored by name, so a stored name outlives its code: after an extension is uninstalled, conversations that select it just stop getting it until it is installed again. `createConversation()`, `fork()`, and `root()` take the same change as `agent`. A task-owned conversation, such as a subagent's, starts as a copy of its owner's conversation's agent. A fork starts with the agent its parent had at the fork entry. The model, prompt, and offered tools of a request are fixed when it is prepared; a change applies from the next request. Tool calls and hooks use the agent as their task phase resolves it, and the environment is built from the current `cwd` at each use, so a `cwd` or extension change can reach calls the model already made.
404:**Turn limits.** `maxTurns` bounds the tool rounds one run takes: the round that reaches it still runs its tools, then the run ends `unanswered` with `turn_limit`, like a failed run (`runFailure`, and `settings.afterFailedRun` decides the inputs queued behind it). An answer always ends the run, so it is never cut off. The count is `docs["pi.live"].run.turns`; it survives restarts, and every run starts at 0, also one a continue starts (see [Busy Conversations](#busy-conversations)), so a host or tool that continues automatically gets a fresh budget each time and must bound that itself. An `onYield` continuation also starts the count over: an extension that keeps a run going bounds its own loop, reading `pi.live.run.turns` and `pi.agent.maxTurns` with `api.snapshot`. `configure()` throws a `RangeError` for anything but an integer 1 or more (or `null`); a change applies at the end of the current round, and children and forks inherit it like the rest of the agent.
422:		documentRetentionMs: 600_000, // loaded documents nothing observes stay in memory this long after their last use; observed ones always
521:Watches are Chord's `WatchHandle` (exported by `@earendil-works/chord/watch`; Durable re-exports it). The listener gets one `WatchCall` per call, tagged by `kind`, and the newest commit's context: `{ kind: "value", value, ops, retired }`. Chord's watches also have `{ kind: "failed", error }` calls for failures a source recovers from; Durable's failures are final, so a Durable watch never sends one and ends instead. A watch ends once, and `closed` (or `stop()`) gives a `WatchEnd` tagged by `kind`: `stopped`, `aborted` (its acquisition context was aborted), `ended` (the Harness closed, or a task document's task ended), `listener_error`, or `failed` (the Harness failed). An ended watch keeps its last value and no longer references the Harness.
523:Adapters that must change in the same step as Durable's memory, such as a Chord source over a view, attach a synchronous observer instead of a watch: `harness.observeConversation(id, observer, context)`, `harness.observeTaskGraph(observer, context)`, and `harness.observeDoc(token, ...scope, observer, context)` return the current `value` and `detach()`, and call `observer.advance(call, context)` with every later change as a `WatchCall` (always a `value` call), unfolded, as memory adopts it: inside the storage's commit for a stored commit, at once for a deferred one (before it is stored). A document observer gets one call per change at its address, so a commit that retires and recreates the document gives two; the task graph also advances without a commit when a registry change alters a task's `blocked` reason. A change can arrive before the `observeXXX()` promise resolves; its ops apply from the observation's `value`. `observer.end(end)` comes once with a `WatchEnd`: `ended` when the Harness closes or, for a task document, right after the retirement of the commit that ends its task, `failed` when the Harness fails. `advance` only records the change: no awaits, no Harness calls, no throws, and quick, since every commit waits for it. One that throws (or returns a rejected promise) is reported, detached, and ended `listener_error`. Watches are built on these observers.
548:    const chat = await tx.doc(MyChat, root.id); // your own conversation document
556:  Call it before the commit's own table writes (`appendEntry()`, `createTask()`, another admission), once per commit: otherwise it throws `ReadAfterWrite`, on every path. Documents may be read and written before and after it. A rejection (`ConversationBusy`, `ConversationIdle`) throws before any write, so you may catch it. Until your commit is stored the admission is provisional: if your callback throws later, no submission exists. Tasks and tools do the same in their own commits through `api.conversation(id)`; entries the admission places carry the task's `byTaskId`.
651:Each field comes from the first layer that sets it: the conversation's `modelOverrides` entry, the settings' `modelOverrides` entry, the conversation's own fields, then the settings. So a model-specific value beats a general one, and the conversation beats the harness at the same level: above, `research` keeps 60000 tokens on a model without an override anywhere and 12000 on `local/small-32k`; without its own entry it would get the settings' 8000 there. The exception is `enabled: false` on the conversation, which also beats the settings' model overrides. A change replaces the stored settings as a whole; `configure()` throws a `RangeError` for a token count that is not an integer 0 or more. Children and forks inherit it like the rest of the agent.
680:Hooks read committed documents (`api.snapshot()`) and the conversation's raw history (`api.entry()`, `api.entries()`), which compaction never changes.
716:## More Conversations and Forks
720:const fork = await root.fork(entryId, { ownership: { kind: "ownerless" } }, context);
723:A fork sees its parent's entries up to `entryId` and continues independently. It keeps the parent's agent as of that entry but receives a fresh provider session identity. Both take `agent` and `init`, applied in the creating commit.
818:Documents are typed JSON objects committed together with entries. Define one, and edit it in a commit:
827:	history: "latest", // or "rewindable" to read old values with snapshotAsOf()
828:	fork: "initial", // what a fork starts with: "initial", "current", or "asOf"
844:		(await tx.doc(Todos, root.id)).items.push("draft"); // a document like Todos: latest, fork "initial"
851:Watches and the view see it at once. Storage gets it with the next commit that reads or writes the document, before the document leaves memory, at close, and at the latest about `withinMs` later; `observeCommits()` sees it then, with exactly what was stored (a document the commit retires shows as its retirement only). A crash loses what was not stored yet, so derive nothing durable from such a value outside a commit that reads it. Only a commit that changes nothing but existing documents with `history: "latest"` and `fork: "initial"` is deferred; anything else in it, such as an entry, a new document, or a task state, stores it at once.
853:`harness.watchDoc()` watches one document like the view above. A document watch follows its address: a retirement is a call with `retired: true`, the last value the watch was given (a change made in the retiring commit itself is not shown), and no ops, and a document created there later arrives as `[["r", value]]` on the same watch. A task document's watch ends `ended` with its task: once the retirement call of the commit that ends the task has returned, or at the Harness's close if that call was never delivered (a watch never started); an observer of it gets `end({ kind: "ended" })` right after the retirement. `HarnessOptions.conversationCreated(tx, conversation)` runs in every commit that creates or forks a conversation, including a tool's raw `tx.createConversation()`, so every conversation gets your documents; `init` in `createConversation()`, `fork()`, and `root()` writes per-call data in the same commit. An extension's tools, sections, and hooks read their own documents through `api` or `input.read`, and treat an absent one as its default ([`10-extension-state.ts`](test/examples/10-extension-state.ts)).
905:A custom backend's `commit(batches, receive, context)` stores `batches` in order, each as one atomic commit with its own seq, checked against the state the earlier ones left, in as few round trips as it can. It stops at the first batch its own checks refuse (global IDs, document actions, copy resolution): the batches before it stay stored, it and the later ones are not. Once they are durable it calls `receive(index, seq)` per stored batch, in order and synchronously, then resolves with `{ stored, refused? }`; it rejects for an unknown outcome (connection lost, a failed statement or `COMMIT`). `receive` is a durability receipt: the Session publishes each batch later, one at a time, so memory and every view never show a partial log. `receive` calls no Storage method and never throws. Custom backends can run the shared conformance suite, which checks this too, with any Vitest- or Jest-compatible runner:
928:2. **An invalid read fails only that call:** a storage read throws `StorageRequestError` for a bad cursor, an unknown conversation, or history a document does not keep. A read you cancelled fails only itself too. The exemption covers reads only: any error from `commit()` or `mintId()`, `StorageRequestError` included, ends the Harness as in rule 1.
